<p>Belarus and Poland carried out a U.S.-brokered prisoner exchange at their border on Tuesday, freeing 10 people in a five-for-five swap that included Polish-Belarusian journalist and activist Andrzej Poczobut. Polish officials said Belarus released three Polish citizens and two Moldovan citizens, while Poland and other participating countries released five people sought by Belarus, Russia and their partners. Poczobut, a correspondent for Gazeta Wyborcza and a member of Belarus’s Polish minority, had been serving an eight-year sentence in a case Poland, the European Union and rights advocates condemned as politically motivated. Poland released Russian archaeologist Alexander Butyagin, whom it had detained at Ukraine’s request over allegations of illegal excavations and damage to cultural heritage in Russian-occupied Crimea. Polish Foreign Minister Radosław Sikorski and Prime Minister Donald Tusk credited U.S. involvement, including President Trump’s special envoy to Belarus, John Coale, while Russian and Belarusian state-linked accounts cited intelligence negotiations involving several countries. Coale said in Warsaw that more than 500 people had been released from Belarusian prisons so far.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/OPpcksXP5gmfXUV3LnGVJqyYy9gQD4eoOGdXH0h3Yih7WHGKWKl_OT-OG5Cg2VrgfS7x9vuSxpi4IgUgXoGYDPhMFFAvfu56SgR4P52Xuq46d3oxGsgpPqf4OwMCQ1f0cxwjlIOgKY1RVPrbOWQpvzUkxi1ZVMaDs9fs0FnstDLWoysKXcIU06RWS1eeVrCy343gtY1KZVtXmsHg8ZGxF2uMPWw' alt='A photograph released by Poland’s government showing Prime Minister Donald Tusk greeting Polish-Belarusian journalist Andrzej Poczobut after his release on the Poland-Belarus border.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Charges in Belarus: Belarusian state media said the people sent to Poland had been punished for alleged espionage against Belarus and Russia.</li><li>Crimea case: Butyagin worked at St. Petersburg’s State Hermitage Museum and headed the Myrmekion archaeological expedition before the exchange.</li><li>Sakharov laureate: Poczobut received the European Parliament’s 2025 Sakharov Prize for Freedom of Thought while imprisoned in Belarus.</li></ul><blockquote>Andrzej Poczobut is free! Welcome to your Polish home, my friend. - Donald Tusk</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Polish government: Prime Minister Donald Tusk said Warsaw’s goal was above all to free Poles and people who had cooperated with Poland, according to Gazeta.pl. (<a href='https://wiadomosci.gazeta.pl/polityka/7,198012,32757563,tusk-o-uwolnieniu-poczobuta-to-jest-sensacyjna-historia.html'>Gazeta.pl</a>)</li><li>Russian Federal Security Service: The FSB said that, in addition to Butyagin, a Russian woman described as the wife of a Russian serviceman in Transnistria returned to Russia. (<a href='https://novayagazeta.ru/articles/2026/04/28/fsb-arkheologa-aleksandra-butiagina-vernuli-v-rossiiu-v-ramkakh-obmena-piat-na-piat-lichnosti-obmeniannykh-ne-raskryvaiutsia-news'>Novaya Gazeta</a>)</li><li>Moldovan government: President Maia Sandu said the return of the two SIS officers could not be measured by a simple mathematical equation, Moldova Live reported. (<a href='https://moldovalive.md/two-sis-officers-released-from-russia-in-exchange-for-balan-former-sis-deputy-chief-pardoned-and-sent-to-belarus/'>Moldova Live</a>)</li><li>Ukrainian government: Foreign Ministry spokesman Heorhii Tykhyi said the case concerned suspected removal of cultural goods from Crimea, not only archaeological activity, Onet reported. (<a href='https://wiadomosci.onet.pl/swiat/wymiana-wiezniow-polska-uwolnila-rosjanina-ukraina-reaguje/sk6tp70'>Onet</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/russias-fsb-says-archaeologist-butyagin-released-by-poland-in-prisoner-swap-tass-reports'>Russia&#x27;s FSB says archaeologist Butyagin released by Poland in prisoner swap, TASS reports</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/28/world/europe/belarus-poland-prisoner-swap-